WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.348
Static Application Security Testing, or SAST, analyzes source code, bytecode, or compiled artifacts to identify security-relevant patterns without requiring a live attack scenario against a running application.

2
00:00:14.348 --> 00:00:24.719
It gives teams a way to inspect implementation choices early, while code is still close to the developer and before risky patterns are packaged into releases.

3
00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:30.021
SAST is not a substitute for thinking, but it is a useful source of early evidence.

4
00:00:30.021 --> 00:00:32.828
SAST is different from related practices.

5
00:00:32.828 --> 00:00:37.117
Secure code review adds human judgment and application context.

6
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:40.470
DAST tests a running application from the outside.

7
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.759
IAST observes behavior during execution with instrumentation.

8
00:00:44.759 --> 00:00:48.736
SCA focuses on third-party components and dependency risk.

9
00:00:48.736 --> 00:00:52.011
Secrets scanning looks for exposed credentials.

10
00:00:52.011 --> 00:00:56.923
Penetration testing evaluates a system from an adversarial perspective.

11
00:00:56.923 --> 00:01:03.162
These practices complement one another because each sees a different part of the risk picture.

12
00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:16.106
Common SAST finding categories include injection risks, insecure cryptography, unsafe deserialization, path handling issues, hardcoded secrets, access control concerns, and unsafe API use.

13
00:01:16.106 --> 00:01:24.995
The tool is usually looking for code patterns, data movement, configuration choices, or framework usage that may indicate a weakness.

14
00:01:24.995 --> 00:01:31.623
A finding should be treated as a lead that requires review, not as an automatic proof of exploitation.

15
00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:35.600
The durable value of SAST is defensive quality improvement.

16
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:46.049
It helps teams find risky code patterns earlier, teach safer implementation habits, and build repeatable review signals into normal engineering workflows.

17
00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:51.586
It cannot guarantee secure software, and it cannot understand every business rule.

18
00:01:51.586 --> 00:01:58.214
Used well, it reduces risk by making security feedback faster, more consistent, and easier to act on.
